# The Passing of Kalaivanar
Tamil title: கலைவாணர் மறைவு

N. S. Krishnan, known as Kalaivanar, died on 30 August 1957. The light of art disappeared and the artistic world grew dark. Anna’s condolence address follows.

## Condolence Address: Opening

A great storm has blown down our Kalaivanar as though it had felled a fine fruit-bearing tree. My tongue will not rise to speak about his rare virtues. There are people here who would gladly give their own lives if doing so could save his.

It cannot be said that Kalaivanar had one private family. Tamil Nadu itself was his family. He belonged to this country and the world of art. His fame spread not only across this subcontinent but throughout the world. There is no Andhra person who did not know him, no one in Kerala who had not heard of him, and no Kannadiga who had not seen or known him.

He travelled beyond India to the innovative land of Russia and won the hearts of its people. Today the world has lost an incomparable artist. Mother Art grieves for her foremost son, and all of us experience a pain we have never before known. The man whose art, service, and good heart elevated the country has departed from it. His loss feels like a death within our own family.

## Inserted Political Speech in Source Images 2–9

The following pages do not continue the condolence address. They contain a separate political speech preserved in the supplied image sequence.

If you know how to honour Tiruppur Kumaran, you too can become a Kumaran. If you do not learn to honour him, who will honour you tomorrow if you are beaten in Chidambaram’s market street during a nonviolent struggle for Dravida Nadu?

Sacrifice does not belong solely to one party or one period. It is an endless serial whose conclusion is unknown. Congressmen wrote one chapter, and we must write the next. If we are to possess the strength to write it, we must respect the chapter already written. I offered this respect even while I belonged to the Dravidar Kazhagam, yet Congressmen became bitter toward me. At election time they abandoned pride, self-respect, and shame to borrow votes from Periyar Ramasami, who had opposed celebrating independence.

I ask comrades of the Dravidian movement to cultivate a spirit of sacrifice. Use these ten years to acquire the strength to recover our motherland; to give statistics to those who demand them, remove suspicion, and teach history to those who have not read it. Every historical and literary work available in Tamil Nadu makes us wonder whether we are truly descendants of that lineage.

Imagine a living tiger entering a mansion where the skin of a hunted tiger hangs upon the wall. If it possessed human understanding, it might look upward and ask: “Were you not once like me, with sharp claws and the power to fight? Why have you become merely a skin?” If the skin could answer, “A killer shot me while I hid in the bushes,” that would resemble Tamil Nadu today. We are like the mounted skin; Tamil history is the living tiger confronting us.

Look at the silted harbours around us. History says ships sailed from them to Rome carrying agarwood and sandalwood from our forests and peacocks that spread their patterned tails in our groves. Did the DMK invent that history? Did Annadurai compose the *Akananuru*, Mathiazhagan the *Purananuru*, or Nedunchezhiyan the *Silappathikaram*? No. When the Tamil had not declined, his intellect had not become clouded, his harbours had not silted, and he maintained fruitful contact with the world.

If we say peacefully, without quarrelling or waging war against the North, that we shall govern such a land as a separate country, what answer is there besides Dandapani Desikar’s line, “Who is the man that says it does not exist?”

People ask where the name Dravida Nadu occurs, posing as great historians. I ask where the name India occurs. Can they show it in the *Ramayana*, *Mahabharata*, *Tevaram*, *Tiruvacakam*, *Akananuru*, *Silappathikaram*, *Civaka Cintamani*, *Tirukkural*, or *Aathichudi*? It is found in none of them.

Once ordinary people recognise a need, they say, “If it is needed, we must obtain it. Who asked whether it is available?” At a wedding, if someone asks whether an eighteen-year-old should marry, the answer comes: “He is eighteen; should he not marry?” If asked where the bride is, they say, “Will there be no woman anywhere? Watch whether I find one!”

When the North-South question takes root in villages, villagers will ask city people: “We now understand North and South. The South must separate from the North. What is the way?” Even if the city says it does not know, villagers will reply, “Whether you know or not, must we not try? Come, let us try.” This will happen within ten years.

Congress initially calculated that the DMK consisted of college-educated people shaped by English civilisation and would remain confined to Coimbatore, Madurai, Madras, Tiruchirappalli, and Dindigul. A musician appears merely to tune the tambura; once the pitch is right, he hands it to the boy beside him and begins to sing. We have finished tuning to some extent and have now begun our song.

Our song tastes sweet in the villages. Villagers listen, grasp its meaning, and understand the issue. The rulers need only recognise this. When Finance Minister Subramaniam asked us to wait ten years, I replied in Madras that we could not accept his request. Those are precisely the years in which we have good work to do.

During those ten years our idea must enter every heart it has not reached. We must discover who among us can become a V. O. Chidambaram Pillai, Bhagat Singh, or Tiruppur Kumaran; who will not hesitate before batons; who sees no difference between home and prison; who will offer fresh blood to recover the motherland; and who can receive the arrows of repression upon the chest without a trace of violent intent. The next generation depends upon that reckoning.

These ten years are a time of urgent work, like milk rising in a pot. A thoughtful mother does not leave the boiling milk even when her child cries; she first takes it off the fire and then lifts the child. In the same way, every Tamil, whatever his occupation, disagreement, or level of Tamil knowledge, must acquire a sacrificial spirit and dedicate himself to recovering the motherland.

That is why I have continued to respect those who sacrificed within Congress. Many were angry with me even when I was in the Dravidar Kazhagam. When independence arrived in 1947, I wrote welcoming it and was condemned. I did so because only those who know how to honour a martyr can themselves become martyrs.

At this point the supplied images jump to a different part of the speech.

The authority to grant Dravida Nadu lies with Pandit Nehru in Delhi, not with Kakkan or Kamaraj here. Their powers extend only to offering a permit when pleased or expelling someone from Congress for a year when angry. We direct our demand toward Delhi, where the authority lies.

Congress ministers miscalculated that village elders would not understand North and South. City audiences may need statistics, Five-Year Plans, Bhakra-Nangal, Hirakud, Sindri, and Chittaranjan explained to them. Villagers need only compare a prosperous Marwari trader they have seen with the impoverished Tamil beside them to understand what we mean by North and South. This is period campaign rhetoric and reflects the speaker’s political framing.

Village elders have long said, “Do not sleep with your head toward the North,” and call the wind from the North harmful. Whatever the older reason, these sayings make the political explanation take hold like cloth dipped into a properly boiling dye vat. City residents may ask whether Dravida Nadu can be obtained. Once the idea enters a villager’s heart, he asks no such question. He knows only that if it is necessary, it must be obtained.

The ministers should understand clearly that every village with a young man of age contains the DMK. It is fortunate for Kamaraj, the speaker jokes, that he never married; had he a son old enough to read an atlas, that son would have understood Anna’s geographical argument and sat here rather than in Congress.

Congress now warns students and young people: “DMK speakers speak beautifully; do not be entranced.” How can such a warning keep people away? If told they speak poorly, people might stay home. Telling them that beautiful speeches await will only make newcomers curious. Perhaps, in this strange world, Congress ministers secretly wish the movement to grow.

Every new school contains fifty future DMK members. Every college grant may yield at least two professors for the movement. Of every hundred people buying the government’s inexpensive *Tirukkural*, perhaps sixty will become interested in the DMK. When flute music is heard in the distance, people turn toward it before asking who plays. In the same manner, the ideal of Dravida Nadu has become a sound filling the air. Closing ears and eyes cannot escape it.

Congress ministers should honestly place before Nehru the reasons advanced for separation and ask him to answer them. Some ministers say, “We shall not give you Dravida Nadu.” This causes both sorrow and embarrassment, because we did not ask them; they possess no authority to give it.

The inserted political sequence ends at this point and does not supply the continuation of that sentence.

## Condolence Address: Conclusion from Image 10

Our Kalaivanar was a good man who attracted every heart. The loss caused by his departure torments us beyond endurance. I believe we can reduce this grief a little only by sharing it with one another.

The departed Kalaivanar filled our hearts and possessed a place in everyone’s mind. He has obtained undying fame. This century has not produced another person like him.

News of his illness spread among the people and shocked the country. When the heartbreaking news of his death followed, people grieved without a path to consolation. The only return we can now make is to set fire to his body. He gave us joy; we give him tears. No other words can remove our suffering.

Kalaivanar had not even completed fifty years. People old enough to be his father remain alive today, yet at so young an age he has left us.

---NOTES---
The source image sequence is discontinuous. Images 1 and 10 form a condolence address for N. S. Krishnan; images 2–9 contain an unrelated Dravida Nadu campaign speech, itself interrupted between images 5 and 6. All available material is translated and explicitly separated rather than silently merged.

The OCR heading gives N. S. Krishnan’s death date as 30 August 1957. The source’s “not even fifty” is consistent with his age but is retained as the speech’s wording rather than independently recalculated.

The inserted speech contains period regional and community generalisations, including a Marwari comparison. They are translated as historical campaign rhetoric and not presented as contemporary claims.
